{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LnupWf3R4tpHHKkFPw57Yo","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, I found the answer in the KIP-1271 discussion threads:\\\\n\\\\n**Encoding adopted:** A **varint** encoding was adopted for the field that records the size of the serialized headers blob.\\\\n\\\\n**What it replaced:** The earlier **2-byte length prefix** (or fixed 2-byte length field) design.\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence:\\\\n\\\\n1. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/13/mail-56a76d3a2f1c08227d88d1f4-ddb97d52.md` (Lucas Brutschy's comment on the KIP):\\\\n   > \\\\\\\"Introducing a 2-byte length prefix artificially limits the header size to 64KB and seems redundant if we are already using a varint for the header count.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n2. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/29/mail-39a5409dbf9aeaf60eed6553-510b70e5.md` (Alieh Saeedi's response):\\\\n   > \\\\\\\"A varint header_size field is introduced to enable lazy deserialization when scanning large ranges.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n3. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/15/mail-96a17a05557028a239b6ad40-caeb580a.md`:\\\\n   > \\\\\\\"we could consider using a varint for the header length to remain flexible and space-efficient\\\\\\\" (as an alternative to \\\\\\\"a fixed-size field for all records\\\\\\\").\\\\n\\\\nThe varint encoding was chosen because it avoids the overhead of a large fixed-size field while remaining space-efficient and consistent with Kafka's serialization standards.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/29/mail-39a5409dbf9aeaf60eed6553-510b70e5.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/15/mail-96a17a05557028a239b6ad40-caeb580a.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/12/github-api-apache-kafka-pr-21297-opened-7f4ea80c.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/13/mail-56a76d3a2f1c08227d88d1f4-ddb97d52.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":34.67,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":1,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":192426,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":15254,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.0632466,\\\"input_tokens\\\":106,\\\"output_tokens\\\":2678}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"297d738e-c666-4213-946a-d751069424eb","uuid":"8df18912-9c0d-419c-a514-6f15fb5ec30e","timestamp":"2026-09-27T18:50:33.205Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, I found the answer in the KIP-1271 discussion threads:\\\\n\\\\n**Encoding adopted:** A **varint** encoding was adopted for the field that records the size of the serialized headers blob.\\\\n\\\\n**What it replaced:** The earlier **2-byte length prefix** (or fixed 2-byte length field) design.\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence:\\\\n\\\\n1. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/13/mail-56a76d3a2f1c08227d88d1f4-ddb97d52.md` (Lucas Brutschy's comment on the KIP):\\\\n   > \\\\\\\"Introducing a 2-byte length prefix artificially limits the header size to 64KB and seems redundant if we are already using a varint for the header count.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n2. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/29/mail-39a5409dbf9aeaf60eed6553-510b70e5.md` (Alieh Saeedi's response):\\\\n   > \\\\\\\"A varint header_size field is introduced to enable lazy deserialization when scanning large ranges.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n3. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/15/mail-96a17a05557028a239b6ad40-caeb580a.md`:\\\\n   > \\\\\\\"we could consider using a varint for the header length to remain flexible and space-efficient\\\\\\\" (as an alternative to \\\\\\\"a fixed-size field for all records\\\\\\\").\\\\n\\\\nThe varint encoding was chosen because it avoids the overhead of a large fixed-size field while remaining space-efficient and consistent with Kafka's serialization standards.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/29/mail-39a5409dbf9aeaf60eed6553-510b70e5.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/15/mail-96a17a05557028a239b6ad40-caeb580a.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/12/github-api-apache-kafka-pr-21297-opened-7f4ea80c.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/13/mail-56a76d3a2f1c08227d88d1f4-ddb97d52.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":34.67,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":1,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":192426,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":15254,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.0632466,\\\"input_tokens\\\":106,\\\"output_tokens\\\":2678}}\"}","_meta":{"fastmcp":{"wrap_result":true}},"structuredContent":{"result":"{\"answer\":\"Based on the context filesystem, I found the answer in the KIP-1271 discussion threads:\\n\\n**Encoding adopted:** A **varint** encoding was adopted for the field that records the size of the serialized headers blob.\\n\\n**What it replaced:** The earlier **2-byte length prefix** (or fixed 2-byte length field) design.\\n\\n## Evidence:\\n\\n1. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/13/mail-56a76d3a2f1c08227d88d1f4-ddb97d52.md` (Lucas Brutschy's comment on the KIP):\\n   > \\\"Introducing a 2-byte length prefix artificially limits the header size to 64KB and seems redundant if we are already using a varint for the header count.\\\"\\n\\n2. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/29/mail-39a5409dbf9aeaf60eed6553-510b70e5.md` (Alieh Saeedi's response):\\n   > \\\"A varint header_size field is introduced to enable lazy deserialization when scanning large ranges.\\\"\\n\\n3. From `/store/_intake/2026/01/15/mail-96a17a05557028a239b6ad40-caeb580a.md`:\\n   > \\\"we could consider using a varint for the header length to remain flexible and space-efficient\\\" (as an alternative to \\\"a fixed-size field for all records\\\").\\n\\nThe varint encoding was chosen because it avoids the overhead of a large fixed-size field while remaining space-efficient and consistent with Kafka's serialization standards.\",\"error\":null,\"files\":[\"store\",\"_intake/2026/01/29/mail-39a5409dbf9aeaf60eed6553-510b70e5.md\",\"_intake/2026/01/15/mail-96a17a05557028a239b6ad40-caeb580a.md\",\"_intake/2026/01/12/github-api-apache-kafka-pr-21297-opened-7f4ea80c.md\",\"_intake/2026/01/13/mail-56a76d3a2f1c08227d88d1f4-ddb97d52.md\"],\"seconds\":34.67,\"truncated\":false,\"turns\":1,\"usage\":{\